1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,119
You can wire controllers, validation,
advice, filters, and uploads and still
ship an API that fights its clients.

2
00:00:08,239 --> 00:00:20,879
REST best practices are the craft layer:
idempotent parcel create, honest
pagination of tracking events, and
boundaries that keep persistence out of
the wire format. Start with resource
naming.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:38,879
Use nouns and plural collections:
/parcels, /parcels/{id},
/parcels/{id}/events. Avoid verby paths
like /createParcel. Let HTTP verbs carry
the action. Keep identifiers opaque in
URLs.

4
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:49,159
Idempotent create matters the moment a
trucker’s mobile app retries a POST after
a timeout. Without an idempotency key, you
register the same parcel twice.

5
00:00:49,280 --> 00:01:03,640
With one, the second call returns the
original resource: Look at this java for a
moment. Store the key with the created
parcel id. On replay, return the same body
and the same Location. Do not invent a
second parcel.

6
00:01:03,759 --> 00:01:13,319
Clients must send the key; document that
requirement as part of the contract.
Pagination keeps tracking-event lists
honest: Look at this java for a moment.

7
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:23,319
Return Page when the UI needs total
counts; return Slice when "has next" is
enough and count queries hurt. Cap size so
a client cannot ask for a million events.

8
00:01:23,439 --> 00:01:35,319
Stable sort keys matter — without them,
page two drifts under concurrent inserts.
DTO boundaries close the loop. Request and
response types are not your JPA entities.

9
00:01:35,439 --> 00:01:47,719
Entities grow lazy associations and
persistence annotations; JSON serializers
will happily walk them into N+1 territory
and leak columns you never meant to
publish. Map explicitly at the edge.

10
00:01:47,840 --> 00:02:03,439
Status discipline stays non-negotiable:
201 for creates, 204 for empty successful
deletes, 404 for missing parcels, 409 for
conflicts, 400 for validation. One error
envelope from your advice keeps mobile
parsers sane.

11
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:16,280
Caching headers on pure GETs of immutable
snapshots can help; do not cache
personalized or rapidly changing gate
state without thought. Prefer ISO-8601
timestamps and explicit money types.

12
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:31,360
Empty collections are 200 with [], not
404. RPC-in-disguise paths
(/parcels/doCheckIn) fight every HTTP tool
you adopt later. Returning entities "just
for now" becomes forever.

13
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:43,560
Skipping idempotency because "retries are
rare" guarantees duplicate parcels on the
first flaky pier Wi-Fi day. Phase Three
gave you the HTTP front door and the
habits to speak it carefully.

14
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:57,360
The next pressure is storage: vessels,
manifests, and cargo lines that must
survive process restarts. Mapping those as
entities — instead of hand-written JDBC
for every vessel column — is where Spring
Data JPA begins.
